Top 15 Scenario-Based Linux Interview Q&A
1. Disk Space Issue
Q: A server reports “No space left on device.” How do you troubleshoot?
A: Use df -h to check filesystem usage, du -sh /path/* for directory sizes, and find / -type f -size +100M to locate large files. Clear logs or move archives to free space.
2. High CPU Usage
Q: A process is consuming 100% CPU. What steps do you take?
A: Run top or htop to identify the process. Use kill -9 PID if it’s stuck, or investigate with strace -p PID to see system calls.
3. Memory Leak
Q: Application memory usage keeps growing. How do you confirm?
A: Use free -m and vmstat. Check per-process memory with ps aux --sort=-%mem. Use valgrind or pmap for deeper analysis.
4. Network Connectivity
Q: Users can’t reach a web app. How do you debug?
A: Check ping, traceroute, and netstat -tulnp. Verify firewall rules (iptables -L or firewalld-cmd). Confirm DNS resolution with dig or nslookup.
5. Slow Boot
Q: System takes too long to boot. How do you investigate?
A: Use systemd-analyze blame to identify slow services. Disable unnecessary services with systemctl disable.
6. File Permission Denied
Q: A user reports “Permission denied” accessing a file.
A: Check ownership with ls -l. Use chmod or chown to correct. Confirm SELinux/AppArmor policies if enabled.
7. SSH Login Failure
Q: SSH login is failing.
A: Check /var/log/secure or /var/log/auth.log. Verify sshd_config settings. Ensure firewall allows port 22.
8. Cron Job Not Running
Q: A scheduled job didn’t execute.
A: Check crontab -l. Verify cron service is running (systemctl status cron). Inspect logs in /var/log/cron.
9. Kernel Panic
Q: Server crashes with kernel panic.
A: Review /var/log/messages. Boot into rescue mode. Check hardware drivers or recent kernel updates.
10. User Account Locked
Q: A user can’t log in due to account lock.
A: Use passwd -S username to check status. Unlock with passwd -u username.
11. Service Down
Q: Apache/Nginx is not responding.
A: Check systemctl status apache2. Review logs in /var/log/httpd/. Test configuration with apachectl configtest.
12. Package Dependency Issue
Q: Installation fails due to dependency errors.
A: Use yum deplist package or apt-cache depends package. Resolve with yum install or apt-get -f install.
13. Firewall Blocking Traffic
Q: Application traffic is blocked.
A: Inspect rules with iptables -L -n. Add exceptions using iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 8080 -j ACCEPT.
14. DNS Resolution Failure
Q: Hostnames don’t resolve.
A: Check /etc/resolv.conf. Test with dig. Restart systemd-resolved.
15. File Corruption
Q: A file is corrupted after transfer.
A: Compare checksums using md5sum or sha256sum. Use rsync --checksum for reliable transfer.
